---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What this site is and who is behind it."
>
  <div class="shell--narrow">
    <div class="page-intro">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>What this is</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm Jacob, a pharmacy student. This site is where I put the calculation
        tools and notes I make while working through the material.
      </p>

      <h2>Why build it</h2>
      <p>
        Pharmacy calculations are not conceptually hard, but they are
        unforgiving. A misplaced decimal or an upside-down conversion factor
        produces an answer that looks perfectly reasonable and is completely
        wrong. The way past that is not memorizing more formulas. It's being
        able to see the setup clearly enough to know when it's wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most calculators online are built for people who already understand the
        problem and just want the arithmetic done. The ones here go the other
        way. They print the whole solution: what the setup looks like, where
        your numbers go, which units cancel, and how the answer falls out. If
        you use one and then find you can do the next problem without it,
        that's the tool working as intended.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <p>
        Mostly me. But a lot of people in my year hit the same walls on the
        basics (proportions, dimensional analysis, percentage strength), and
        those walls tend to stay up because everyone is too busy to go back and
        rebuild the foundation. If you're in that position, these are aimed
        squarely at you.
      </p>

      <h2>A word of caution</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here is a study aid written by a student. I've been careful,
        but careful is not the same as authoritative. Check anything that
        matters against your course material, an official reference, and a
        pharmacist. Don't let a calculator on a personal website be the last
        thing standing between a number and a patient.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
